cff-version: 1.2.0
title: NEMSweep
message: >-
  If you use NEMSweep in work you publish, please cite it. Citation is how a
  method gets scrutinised rather than just used.
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Hasintha
    family-names: Attanayake
    # orcid: 'https://orcid.org/0000-0000-0000-0000'
repository-code: 'https://github.com/HasinthaAttanayake/NEMSweep'
url: 'https://www.nemsweep.com/'
abstract: >-
  An hourly grid dispatch model for Australia's National Electricity Market.
  Regions are described with their demand, generation assets and storage, then
  dispatched hour by hour across a modelled year; where a declared reliability
  standard is not met, storage is grown until it is met or the search reports
  what stopped it. Deterministic: the same inputs at the same commit reproduce
  every modelled value, and every result records the SHA-256 of the input bytes
  it was built from.
keywords:
  - electricity
  - National Electricity Market
  - dispatch model
  - energy systems
  - renewable energy
  - storage sizing
  - Australia
license: BSD-3-Clause
# No DOI yet, and that is a decision rather than an omission.
#
# Minting one means archiving a snapshot of this repository permanently and
# publicly, and the snapshot would include the demand, generation and weather
# artifacts under NEMSweep.Web/wwwroot/data. Those derive from AEMO and
# EnergyPlus Weather sources whose redistribution terms are still open; see
# DATA-LICENSE.md. An archive is deliberately hard to withdraw, so it is the
# wrong place to resolve that question after the fact.
#
# When the terms are settled, the fields below complete the record. Archiving a
# tree without the data artifacts is also a reasonable answer, and arguably the
# better one: what is worth citing is the model, not one illustrative run.
#
#   doi: '10.5281/zenodo.XXXXXXX'
#   version: '0.1.0'
#   date-released: 'YYYY-MM-DD'
